WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.029
Film Courage: Jeff you say making a movie
is like starting a business?

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:05.029
Why make that comparison?

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:06.819
Jeff Deverett, film producer, entrepreneur,
writer and director: Making an independent

00:00:06.819 --> 00:00:07.819
movie.

00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:10.500
There are so much to it.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.330
I mean there are so many components to making
a movie.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:20.330
Think of you’re creating something from
scratch, from an idea and that’s what a

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:21.330
business is.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.960
You have an idea and you want to bring it
to life and bring whatever it is your product

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.631
or service to market and you have to go through
all of those steps and components to do that.

00:00:29.631 --> 00:00:32.730
That is what making a movie is.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:41.440
Ultimately you’re going to develop it, produce
it and then market it and so that’s what

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.050
a business is, it’s creating a product or
a service and then putting it out to market

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:51.390
and then hoping that consumers consume it
and in the case of movie the consumption is

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:55.870
watching it or buying it whatever in that
case.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:57.180
How is that any different than any business?

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.620
That’s what all businesses do, they create
from an idea, a product or a service and put

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:05.810
it out to market to be consumed and that’s
why I relate it to a business.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:10.240
Now in the studios there are two types.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.560
I believe that the studio business and the
independent business are entirely two separate

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:22.160
businesses the way a public company would
be different from an entrepreneurial company.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.880
So let’s deal with the public company so
a public company has shareholders and they

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:32.390
have a responsibility and a mandate to earn
revenue and dividends or whatever for their

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.980
shareholders, that’s their mission.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:41.409
Their mission is to create wealth so that
they can distribute it to their shareholders.

00:01:41.409 --> 00:01:48.659
So everybody is focused on that, on wealth
creation and whatever it takes to be profitable,

00:01:48.659 --> 00:01:56.100
that’s why corporations exists especially
public corporations so that’s what a studio

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.190
is.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:02.790
I mean every studio is a public company so
every studio has to be financially successful

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:03.790
for two reasons.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.951
One if they’re not they can’t exist and
make products they want to do and two their

00:02:06.951 --> 00:02:08.920
shareholders wouldn’t support it.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:14.100
So the studios are public companies and they
are financial institutions that create wealth.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.430
That’s the way I look at them.

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:20.390
The product they happen to be making is entertainment,
TV shows, whatever.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:24.470
But it’s for the reason of generating profit.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:29.400
There are not many studio people I have met
who will say to you I want to do this because

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.360
this is what I like and enjoy and it doesn’t
matter if they make profit.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.879
They won’t last too long in the studio system.

00:02:34.879 --> 00:02:37.420
They have to be profit oriented.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:43.670
On the entrepreneurial side which I would
relate to the indie filmmaking business you

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:49.680
also if you want to exist and continue you
do have to make a profit like any business

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:55.630
but you can take more risks the stakes are
not lower and you’re not accountable to

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:58.959
anybody other than yourself and your investors.

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:01.760
On the public company side you’re accountable
to all of your shareholders.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.390
Here the accountability factor is less.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:08.060
So you don’t want to stress your brains
out and lose all of your investors money or

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:09.060
whoever it is.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.989
It might just be your own money and your own
time.

00:03:10.989 --> 00:03:16.340
But you have the ability to take more risks
which is what an entrepreneur does.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:21.260
Maybe have a little bit more fun, maybe go
a little bit more passion, do the things that

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.330
you truly want to do.

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:29.020
When I speak to filmmakers the two questions
they always ask me is what genre film should

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:31.250
I make that would be most saleable?

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:38.050
That’s question number one and I answer
that question and I say great question but

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.959
let me ask you a question before I answer
your question.

00:03:40.959 --> 00:03:43.310
What genre film do you like to make?

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:46.500
See in a studio you would never have that
discussion.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.250
That would be we need to make, here is what
is trending, here is what is happening in

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:53.560
the marketplace, here is what we’re forecasting,
this is where we are going to make the most

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:55.140
amount of money.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.590
Nobody says “Hey would do you think we should
do?

00:03:57.590 --> 00:03:58.890
What would be the most fun?

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.780
What would be the most artistic?”

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:05.599
In the entrepreneurial world you can have
that conversation, art actually matters.

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:08.519
I mean you know listen in the studio world
you have to make good product.

00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:13.510
It has to be fantastic and very artistic in
order for people to be entertained by it.

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:16.719
But they don’t have the luxury of picking
and choosing the way an entrepreneur does

00:04:16.719 --> 00:04:21.249
because again they’re accountable to a smaller
group of people.

00:04:21.249 --> 00:04:25.889
But on the business side (why I related it
to business) is because when you decide to

00:04:25.889 --> 00:04:34.110
make a movie from start to finish if you want
to be what I call a career filmmaker who makes

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:41.250
just more than one movie it would be helpful
if you were profitable because that way 1)

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:45.569
You’d have enough money to make your money
back which would help to make your next movie

00:04:45.569 --> 00:04:48.699
2) That would invoke investor confidence.

00:04:48.699 --> 00:04:53.529
If you are dealing with investors they would
say hey this guy is able to make money with

00:04:53.529 --> 00:04:58.569
the stuff we’re investing in so it does
make it a lot easier if you’re profitable

00:04:58.569 --> 00:05:03.749
on the entrepreneurial side but you don’t
have to necessarily be profitable because

00:05:03.749 --> 00:05:10.129
it might not lead to a good career but you
could be a hobby filmmaker, just an artist

00:05:10.129 --> 00:05:13.802
that says I have a statement that I want to
make to the world and I’m going to do it

00:05:13.802 --> 00:05:16.199
through a movie and it’s important for me.

00:05:16.199 --> 00:05:18.229
This is the kind of movie I want to make.

00:05:18.229 --> 00:05:22.439
This is the artistic integrity and I don’t
really want to compromise so I am not really

00:05:22.439 --> 00:05:25.009
worried about what the audience thinks.

00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:27.310
I am more worries about what I think.

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:32.419
I personally don’t think that makes for
great business practice because it might not

00:05:32.419 --> 00:05:38.029
generate the profit that you’re looking
for but an entrepreneur gets to do that.

00:05:38.029 --> 00:05:43.889
That’s part of being in business and if
you look at the statistics of entrepreneurs

00:05:43.889 --> 00:05:46.490
most small businesses fail miserably.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:52.360
It’s really, really tough to navigate opening
a new business.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.229
That would be the same with indie films.

00:05:55.229 --> 00:05:59.819
The good news about making indie film is even
though you didn’t make any money and you

00:05:59.819 --> 00:06:05.050
could say it sort of failed financially, it
didn’t necessarily fail artistically.

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:09.409
So you’re going to leave the world with
your legacy and your message and whatever

00:06:09.409 --> 00:06:11.389
and hopefully audiences will enjoy it.

00:06:11.389 --> 00:06:13.909
Maybe they didn’t pay for it but hopefully
they will enjoy it.

00:06:13.909 --> 00:06:16.009
So at least you get that out of it.

00:06:16.009 --> 00:06:19.879
Whereas a failed business on the entrepreneurial
side you know unless they open a restaurant

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:23.699
or a clothing store or something like that
and that fails you’re just left with a lot

00:06:23.699 --> 00:06:26.270
of debt and a lot of aggravation.

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:31.680
Film Courage: I was actually going to bring
up as the next thing regarding certain businesses

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:34.610
and I was wondering if we can comparison to
certain types of movies.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:39.159
So if you have a restaurant which is what
90% failure rate?

00:06:39.159 --> 00:06:42.740
I’m not sure if that is the right percentage?

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:47.800
Versus let’s say someone selling widgets…it’s
LED lights and it’s all the rage.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:53.050
Are certain movies in the category of restaurants
and certain movies are in the categories of

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:54.050
widgets?

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:59.889
Jeff: I would look at it a little differently
because certain restaurants in certain locations

00:06:59.889 --> 00:07:04.770
I think have a better chance than other restaurants
in other locations.

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:10.529
So I would say certain…there’s a trend
in the movie business.

00:07:10.529 --> 00:07:17.610
As you know like sometimes horror movies are
the hot rage, sometimes it’s sci-fi and

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:21.929
definitely if you’re in it to make money,
if you’re in the film business to make money

00:07:21.929 --> 00:07:26.300
you definitely want to consider what your
audience wants to see and who your audience

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:29.550
actually is and that was the second question.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:33.560
They always ask “What film should I make?”

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:37.619
And the second question is “How do I get
it to the audience?”

00:07:37.619 --> 00:07:41.939
The first way to get a film to an audience
is to identity who the audience is which means

00:07:41.939 --> 00:07:46.719
you have to choose the genre of the film that
you’re going to aim at your audience.

00:07:46.719 --> 00:07:49.729
So for example I tend to make family films.

00:07:49.729 --> 00:07:54.319
I like family films, I enjoy making them,
I enjoy the genre, I have four kids, they

00:07:54.319 --> 00:07:58.089
were growing up, it was a nice thing for me
to do but I did it because I really enjoy

00:07:58.089 --> 00:08:00.369
family films.

00:08:00.369 --> 00:08:04.819
And then within family films I am very specific
my films are very specific they are generally

00:08:04.819 --> 00:08:13.039
sports dramas based on true stories because
I love that genre and it’s very, very marketable

00:08:13.039 --> 00:08:17.479
especially each one has a defined market.

00:08:17.479 --> 00:08:22.499
Like I made two gymnastics films so I can
go to the gymnastics world.

00:08:22.499 --> 00:08:28.659
I am able to actually target market gymnasts
and dancers so I don’t have to go generically

00:08:28.659 --> 00:08:31.639
across the whole gambit to try and market
to everybody.

00:08:31.639 --> 00:08:37.180
I can specifically hone my marketing efforts
in on gymnasts and dancers who I would think

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:42.760
would be engaged by the movie (by these particular
movies) which has worked.

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:48.620
So as a business decision it does help to
identify the audience that you want to go

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:54.760
to and then choose the movie that will hopefully
cater to that audience or vice versa if you’re

00:08:54.760 --> 00:09:00.150
going to choose a movie make sure you identify
how you’re going to who the audience is

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:05.520
and how you’re going to market to them before
you make the movie otherwise it’s hard to

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:07.420
do it after the fact.

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:09.280
This is what studios do.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:13.030
I mean I’m convinced (I’ve never worked
at a studio) but I am 100 percent convinced

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:16.630
that if the marketing department doesn’t
green light the movie then the production

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:18.000
department is not making it.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:21.660
They need to know who it’s going to, how
it’s going to get marketed, what the plan

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:27.360
is going to be in order to monetize the product
they make and indie film producers generally

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:32.620
don’t do that but if they did do that then
kind of like a restauranteur opening up a

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:33.620
restaurant.

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:42.280
If you’re going to open say an Indian restaurant
in an Asian marketplace it could be tricky?

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:43.280
I don’t know?

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.820
Maybe Asian people like Indian food?

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:49.460
But you better do your research and make sure
they do otherwise your restaurant could be

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:52.500
fantastic, it just might be in the wrong place.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:55.010
And that’s the same with the film.

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:59.300
If you’re going to make a film that caters
to a certain audience and you haven’t really

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:02.600
identified who the audience is or how to get
to them, you could make the greatest film

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.640
of all time but it’s going to be hard to
get it to your audience.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:09.770
Film Courage: You know you had mentioned earlier
(and I love this point that you’re making)

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:14.920
about clothing and it brings back something
I watched about Diane von Fürstenberg and

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:21.130
her wrap dress which was all the rage which
I think it was late 1970’s/1980’s and

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:25.340
then I think she got in trouble because you
bought a new inventory because her dresses

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:28.690
were so popular but by that time tastes had
changed if I remember correctly.

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:32.370
So she had all this inventory that was not
selling at the time.

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:37.000
I think her dresses made a resurgence later
but do audiences…that’s the thing.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:43.840
If we know the rage is horror movies with
a certain soundtrack and things, can the audience

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:47.670
change and now you have this excess surplus?

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:52.340
Jeff: I have maybe a skewed opinion about
that.

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:59.260
So I believe that anything that is unique,
good and really entertaining can actually

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:00.860
create a trend itself.

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:06.400
And can find an audience and it’s funny
I notice over the years especially with studio

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:12.480
pictures (it’s always with studio movies)
trends seem to develop like in the 1970’s

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:18.360
you had this STAR WARS, STAR TREK, all these
star space themes right?

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:32.640
That doesn’t just come 

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:44.140
out 

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:46.500
of nowhere.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:14:17.740
So I have always thought about like how does

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:58.900
a trend 

00:14:58.900 --> 00:15:01.050
like that develop?

